17.What is the Use of the PATH Variable?
->The PATH environment variable is used by the operating system to locate executable programs. In Java, setting the PATH variable allows you to run Java
commands such as java, javac, jar, and javadoc from any directory in the command prompt or terminal without specifying their full installation path.
Example
If the PATH variable is set correctly, you can simply run:
javac Hello.java
java Hello
Instead of: C:\Program Files\Java\jdk-21\bin\javac Hello.java

18.What is the Use of the CLASSPATH Variable?
-> The CLASSPATH is an environment variable that tells the JVM and Java compiler where to find user-defined classes, packages, and external library files (JAR files) 
required by a Java program. If a required class or library is not found in the CLASSPATH, Java throws a ClassNotFoundException or NoClassDefFoundError.
Example
Suppose your project uses an external library mysql-connector.jar.
You can include it in the classpath like this:
javac -cp .;lib/mysql-connector.jar MyProgram.java
java -cp .;lib/mysql-connector.jar MyProgram

19.How Do You Assign Values to PATH and CLASSPATH Variables?
-> The PATH and CLASSPATH variables are configured as environment variables in the operating system. The PATH variable is set to the JDK's bin directory so Java commands like java and javac can be executed from any location. The CLASSPATH variable is set to the directories containing Java class files and external JAR files so that the JVM and compiler can locate them.
  Setting PATH (Windows):-
Open System Properties → Advanced → Environment Variables.
Under System Variables, select Path → Edit.
Add the JDK bin directory, for example:
C:\Program Files\Java\jdk-21\bin
Save the changes and restart the Command Prompt.
  Setting CLASSPATH (Windows):-
Create a new environment variable:
Variable Name: CLASSPATH
Variable Value:
.;C:\Program Files\Java\jdk-21\lib
Or include your project folder and JAR files:
.;C:\MyProject\classes;C:\MyProject\lib\mysql-connector.jar
. represents the current working directory.
Verify the Settings
java -version
javac -version
echo %PATH%
echo %CLASSPATH%

20.What are the Characteristic Features of Java?
-> Java is a high-level, object-oriented, secure, robust, and platform-independent programming language. It is designed with features that make it reliable, portable, and suitable for developing enterprise, web, mobile, and desktop applications. The main characteristics of Java are:
Simple – Java has a clean syntax and removes complex features like pointers and operator overloading.
Object-Oriented – Everything is based on classes and objects, supporting encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction.
Platform Independent – Java follows "Write Once, Run Anywhere (WORA)" by compiling source code into bytecode, which runs on any platform with a JVM.
Portable – Java programs can be moved from one platform to another without modification.
Secure – Java provides security through the JVM, Bytecode Verifier, Class Loader, and the absence of explicit pointers.
Robust – Java has strong memory management, exception handling, and automatic garbage collection, making applications reliable.
Multithreaded – Java supports concurrent execution of multiple threads, improving performance and responsiveness.
Distributed – Java provides built-in support for networking, making it suitable for distributed applications.
High Performance – The JIT (Just-In-Time) Compiler optimizes frequently executed code, improving runtime performance.
Dynamic – Java can load classes dynamically at runtime using the Class Loader.
Architecture Neutral – Java bytecode is independent of the underlying processor architecture.
Interpreted and Compiled – Java source code is compiled into bytecode by javac, and the JVM interprets or JIT-compiles it during execution.

21.In which interface we can find the interface of foreach method?
->here is no foreach method in Java. The enhanced for loop (for-each loop) is a language feature, not a method. It can be used with arrays and any object that implements the Iterable interface.
The Iterable interface provides the following method:
Iterator<T> iterator();
The enhanced for-each loop internally uses this iterator() method to traverse the collection.
Example
import java.util.ArrayList;
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("Java");
        list.add("Python");
        list.add("C++");
        for (String language : list) {
            System.out.println(language);
        }
    }
}

22.When Do We Prefer Parallel Sort Over Serial Sort?
-> We prefer parallelSort() over sort() when sorting large datasets on a multi-core processor. parallelSort() divides the array into smaller parts,
sorts them simultaneously using multiple threads (Fork/Join framework), and then merges the results. This improves performance for large arrays.
For small datasets, sort() is generally preferred because the overhead of creating and managing multiple threads can make parallelSort() slower.










